{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on Popper's terms as a non-inductive, methodological decision to act on the theory that has withstood the most severe attempts at falsification, thereby maximizing verisimilitude without assigning probability.","counterconditions":["If 'verisimilitude' cannot be defined or assessed without probabilistic assumptions, rendering the decision rule circular.","If the distinction between 'acting on' a theory and 'believing' it to be probable collapses under pragmatic analysis."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Methodological Decision Rule: Scientists adopt the best-tested theory not because they believe it is true or probable, but because it is the most rational choice given the aim of science (truth). This is a volitional act aligned with the goal of finding truth-likeness (verisimilitude), distinct from an inductive inference that the future will resemble the past.","optional_refs":["1db4bfb4cb832e10e8377fcd3ca4efea4051f10d7c176eb543ad07f9bc872906","1da20c8528cb96efcdb315de18ed584bfed95d4e74109bbbde9522acbff3d465"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for better-corroborated theories inevitably smuggles induction back into Popper's system because 'severe testing' and 'survival' implicitly rely on the inductive assumption that the future will resemble the past regarding the validity of tests and the stability of natural laws.","counterconditions":["If severity can be defined purely logically or synchronically without reference to temporal stability.","If the preference can be grounded solely in the logical content of the theory independent of its testing history."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Hidden Inductive Premise: The concept of a 'severe test' presupposes that the conditions under which a theory was tested are representative of future conditions. Without assuming the uniformity of nature (that what held yesterday holds tomorrow), the survival of a theory today offers no logical reason to prefer it for action tomorrow.","optional_refs":["7b79dc85202f082956e18e49919e23b4fb1ef3037d5b3a9a1fcf4d7b7af79ab4","e362217272b0db549f088a339c721075c6a44c6cd31ba50297e59039517f0132"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.82},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The surviving resolution is that the preference is defensible as a 'critical preference' based on the logical asymmetry of falsification, but accepting this costs us the comfort of believing our chosen theory is likely true; we must act on it while acknowledging it is likely false.","counterconditions":["If the logical asymmetry of falsification does not translate into a practical guide for action in complex, underdetermined scenarios.","If scientists cannot psychologically sustain action on theories they explicitly believe are likely false."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Asymmetric Rationality: We prefer the better-corroborated theory not because it is probable, but because it is the only one that has not been logically contradicted by evidence. The cost is a radical fallibilism where action is taken on theories known to be potentially false, with no expectation of final truth, only temporary survival.","optional_refs":["e53dc321c83c7687f43a9d937ec7080f215fec4983c62fb03a317014bfe0c2ff","e362217272b0db549f088a339c721075c6a44c6cd31ba50297e59039517f0132"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.65},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The tension dissolves if we view corroboration reports not as guides for theory choice, but as historical records of criticism; the 'preference' is actually a pragmatic convention of the scientific community that operates outside Popper's strict logical framework, effectively admitting a limited, localized induction.","counterconditions":["If Popper's writings explicitly forbid any form of pragmatic induction, even as a convention.","If the 'preference' can be fully derived from logical principles without resorting to sociological conventions."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Pragmatic Convention: Popper's logical system prohibits induction, but the practice of science requires it. The 'preference' is a sociological tool for consensus-building that implicitly accepts a 'principle of limited induction' (the future will resemble the past until proven otherwise) which Popper verbally denied but practically tolerated.","optional_refs":["74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","7b79dc85202f082956e18e49919e23b4fb1ef3037d5b3a9a1fcf4d7b7af79ab4"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.45}],"simulation_proposals":[]}